Subject: [PATCH] fix(rocr): throttle AsyncEventsLoop polling-fallback spin on
 WSL and mixed batches (closes ROCm/librocdxg#60) (#7898)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

## Problem

When `Runtime::AsyncEventsLoop` (`core/runtime/runtime.cpp`) cannot arm
an interrupt-backed wait, it falls back to polling: `PrepareInterrupt()`
returns `false`, the loop sets `polling = true`, and the inner loop
re-scans signal values in userspace. In that mode the loop had **no
sleep**, so each `AsyncEventsLoop` thread monopolized a CPU core for the
entire lifetime of its async-events context.

The polling fallback is reachable in two distinct situations:

1. **Globally** — the thunk exposes no interrupt-backed signal events at
all (`g_use_interrupt_wait == false`): WSL2/dxg (where `librocdxg` stubs
`hsaKmtCreateEvent`), DTIF, KFD 1.0, or `HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=0`. Every
signal forces polling. On WSL2 any process that has merely touched the
GPU burns **~2 host CPU cores** while completely idle — reported and
root-caused in ROCm/librocdxg#60.
2. **Per wait batch, on ordinary interrupt-capable Linux** — a single
registered signal with no `EopEvent()` (an IPC signal registered via
`hsa_amd_signal_async_handler`, which explicitly permits `IPCSignal`, or
an internal `DefaultSignal` such as gang-copy signals) forces the
*entire* batch into polling, dragging unrelated interrupt-backed
handlers on the shared thread with it. This pre-existing behavior was
identified during review ([expert-agent
review](https://github.com/ROCm/rocm-systems/pull/7898#pullrequestreview-4856707566));
earlier revisions of this PR wrongly claimed the branch was unreachable
on interrupt-backed platforms.

## Fix

Nap between scans in the forced-polling branch with an escalating
backoff: **20 µs doubling up to a ceiling**, re-initialized at every new
wait batch so escalation only compounds within a single idle wait. The
ceiling depends on *why* the loop is polling:

- **Interrupts globally unavailable** (`g_use_interrupt_wait == false`):
ceiling **2 ms** (`kPollNapCeilingUs`). Every signal is polling-only, so
the only cost of a long nap is the napping wait's own observation
latency.
- **Mixed batch on an interrupt-capable system** (`g_use_interrupt_wait
== true`): ceiling **200 µs** (`kPollNapCeilingMixedUs`). The nap here
delays *unrelated* interrupt-backed handlers on the shared thread, so it
is capped at the same 200 µs the interrupt path already spends in its
active-poll window before `hsaKmtWaitOnMultipleEvents` — the thread's
pre-existing latency noise floor.

```cpp
const int poll_nap_ceiling_us =
    g_use_interrupt_wait ? kPollNapCeilingMixedUs : kPollNapCeilingUs;
...
} else if (polling && !finish) {
  os::uSleep(poll_nap_us);
  poll_nap_us = NextPollNapUs(poll_nap_us, poll_nap_ceiling_us);
}
```

Pure interrupt-backed batches take the `WaitForInterrupt()` (KFD sleep)
path and never enter this branch — verified by measurement below.
Kernel-completion waits use the separate userspace `BusyWaitSignal` path
and are likewise untouched.

## Measurements

**WSL2** — RX 7900 XTX (gfx1100), ROCm 7.2.4, native `libhsa-runtime64`
swapped via `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`:

| scenario | stock | patched |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| idle with a model/context resident | **182%** CPU | **1.07%** CPU |
| 200 back-to-back kernel + `hipDeviceSynchronize` iterations | 196%
CPU, 0.22 s | **44% CPU, 0.23 s** |

Wall-clock for back-to-back work is unchanged (within noise) because
every new wait resets to the 20 µs floor.

**Native Linux, mixed batch** — Ryzen 9950X3D, Radeon AI PRO R9700
(gfx1201) + RX 7900 XTX (gfx1100), interrupts enabled, stock KFD.
Interrupt-backed async-handler callback latency over 300 sparse
completions while a never-satisfied IPC-signal handler is parked on the
same async-events thread:

| build | idle CPU (async thread) | callback latency p50 / p99 / max |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| pre-PR spin | 99.9% of a core | 0.2 / 0.7 / 1.8 µs |
| 2 ms cap (earlier revision of this PR) | 0.2% | 1219 / 2046 / 2053 µs
|
| **this PR (200 µs mixed cap)** | 0.6% | **119 / 249 / 251 µs** |

Pure interrupt-backed batches are unchanged in all three builds (p50 ≈
10 µs, 0% idle CPU).

## Reproduction

A 20-line HIP program that touches the GPU once and then sleeps
reproduces the spin on stock ROCm under WSL2 (full source in
ROCm/librocdxg#60); with this patch the same program idles at ~1% CPU
instead of ~182%. The native-Linux mixed-batch case is reproduced by
registering `hsa_amd_signal_async_handler` callbacks on one
interrupt-backed signal plus one IPC-attached signal and timing the
interrupt signal's callbacks.

## Testing

The nap escalation is factored into a small dependency-free helper
(`core/util/poll_backoff.h`: `kPollNapFloorUs`, `kPollNapCeilingUs`,
`kPollNapCeilingMixedUs`, `NextPollNapUs`) and covered by
`rocrtst/common/utils_test/poll_backoff_test.cpp` (gtest): bound
ordering (floor < mixed < full), the doubling escalation, saturation and
fixed-point behavior at both ceilings, and the per-wait reset to the
floor. All 5 cases pass.

Closes ROCm/librocdxg#60

